#!RuleTest
#test PMDA-SD1122 expect=violation domain=LB
#note "Rows 1-2 (plain and leading-zero decimals) carry numeric LBSTRESC with LBSTRESN empty -> fire. ⭐ ROW 3 FIRES AGAIN as of 2026-09-14 (count 2 -> 3), and this is a RESTORATION, not a new behaviour. The history in three steps, because the middle one is what this note used to record: the rule was authored with /^[+-]?(\\d+(\\.\\d+)?|\\.\\d+)([eE][+-]?\\d+)?$/, which ACCEPTS exponents, so '1.23E-4' always was a numeric result whose missing LBSTRESN is a defect; wave TA-4 swapped that regex for is_numeric, whose hand-rolled scan rejected exponents, and the finding was lost — re-pinned here as 'probe delta of record'; engine ef60470 made is_numeric parse-based (owner ruling 2026-09-14: 'even string values with exponents are numeric values'), which puts the rule back where it was authored. ⚠ A numeric lab result is routinely written in exponent form — that is what --STRESC is FOR — so this row is the rule's core case, not an edge one."
#library standard=sdtmig version=3-4
#expectViolationCount 3
#expectViolationAt row=1 LBSTRESC=5.5
#expectViolationAt row=2 LBSTRESC=007
#expectViolationAt row=3 LBSTRESC=1.23E-4
dataset LB class=FINDINGS label="Laboratory Test Results"
col STUDYID  type=Char label="Study Identifier"
col DOMAIN   type=Char label="Domain Abbreviation"
col USUBJID  type=Char label="Unique Subject Identifier"
col LBSEQ    type=Num  label="Sequence Number"
col LBTESTCD type=Char label="Lab Test or Examination Short Name"
col LBSTRESC type=Char label="Character Result/Finding in Std Format"
col LBSTRESN type=Num  label="Numeric Result/Finding in Standard Units"
---
S1 | LB | 001 | 1 | GLUC | 5.5     |
S1 | LB | 001 | 2 | ALB  | 007     |
S1 | LB | 001 | 3 | TSH  | 1.23E-4 |
